Original paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/natural_sciences_tripos_part_1a/response/3284364/attach/3/FOI%202025%201239%20Hagendoorn%20materials.pdf?cookie_passthrough=1#page=25[Public original PDF, physical pages 25–33; question pages 26–33].

= 1
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <Bravais lattice> is a set of equivalent points generated by integer combinations of three independent translation vectors. A <crystal motif> is the arrangement of atoms attached identically to every <lattice point>. Together they specify the periodic <crystal structure>: lattice translations repeat the motif without changing its internal arrangement.

A <unit cell> is a region whose translations tile the structure. A <primitive unit cell> contains one <lattice point> after sharing boundary points with neighboring cells. A conventional cell can contain more than one <lattice point> to display the crystal's symmetry with simple axes and angles; for example, a conventional face-centered cube has four lattice points but a less visually symmetric primitive cell has one.

**Rotational symmetry compatible with a periodic three-dimensional lattice has order $1,2,3,4$ or $6$**, as expressed by the <crystallographic restriction theorem>. A crystallographic fivefold axis is excluded from an ordinary periodic crystal. This statement concerns exact lattice symmetry; it does not exclude local fivefold atomic arrangements or nonperiodic ordered structures.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <body-centered tetragonal lattice> has points at $(0,0,0)$ and $(1/2,1/2,1/2)$ in the conventional cell. Add the two-atom motif to each. Reducing coordinates modulo integer translations gives
$$
\boxed{(0,0,0),\quad(0,1/2,1/4),\quad
(1/2,1/2,1/2),\quad(1/2,0,3/4).}
$$
Thus the cell contains **four atoms**, not merely the two lattice points. In projection along $[001]$, label each projected point by its fractional height $z/c$; the four sites do not all lie in the same plane.

\Image[paper-1-tin-projection.png]
{title=Original calculated $[001]$ projection of two-by-two conventional beta-tin cells; colors and labels give fractional height, not different chemical species}

The plotted repeat is $a=0.5831\,\mathrm{nm}$ in both horizontal directions. Atoms on the outer drawing boundary are repeated images needed to show neighboring cells, rather than additional unshared atoms in the four-cell counting volume.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The metallic conventional cell has four tin atoms, whereas the conventional <diamond cubic structure> cell has eight. Therefore the volume per atom is
$$
v_\beta=\frac{(0.5831)^2(0.3182)}4
=0.0270474\,\mathrm{nm^3},
\qquad
v_\alpha=\frac{(0.6489)^3}{8}
=0.0341541\,\mathrm{nm^3}.
$$
The fractional change for metallic to nonmetallic tin is
$$
\boxed{\frac{v_\alpha-v_\beta}{v_\beta}\simeq0.2627,
\qquad\text{about }26.3\%\text{ expansion}.}
$$
The atom count is unchanged, so this is also the unconstrained bulk volume change for complete transformation under the model. If it were macroscopically isotropic, the corresponding free linear expansion would be $(1.2627)^{1/3}-1\simeq8.1\%$.

Such a large transformation <strain> creates stresses where transformed and untransformed regions constrain one another. Cracking, loss of mechanical integrity and disintegration can follow: the phenomenon is <tin pest>. The calculation establishes the volume mismatch, not the transformation rate; <temperature>, nucleation and impurities affect when a real specimen changes.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**The 020 reflection is extinguished by the diamond basis, even though it satisfies the face-centered lattice parity rule.** Write the diamond structure as an FCC lattice with identical atoms at basis positions $(0,0,0)$ and $(1/4,1/4,1/4)$.

The <structure factor> separates into lattice and basis terms:
$$
F_{hkl}=f\left[1+(-1)^{h+k}+(-1)^{h+l}+(-1)^{k+l}\right]
\left[1+e^{i\pi(h+k+l)/2}\right].
$$
At $(h,k,l)=(0,2,0)$, all indices are even, so the first bracket is $4$. The second bracket is $1+e^{i\pi}=0$. The two identical sublattices scatter with opposite phases and cancel:
$$
\boxed{F_{020}=0,\qquad I_{020}\propto|F_{020}|^2=0.}
$$
For all-even reflections of diamond, the additional allowed condition is $h+k+l$ divisible by four. This is a <systematic absence>, not an absence of the corresponding family of geometrical planes.

= 2
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <voltage> is a mechanically mediated effect: <nickel> <magnetostriction> strains the bonded central layer, and the direct <piezoelectric effect> converts <stress> into electrical displacement. **An estimate of $|d_{31}|$ requires a strain-transfer and electrical-boundary assumption.**

Let $x$ run along the applied field and $z$ through the layers. Assume saturated, approximately volume-preserving isotropic <nickel> <magnetostriction>, perfect bonding, mechanically dominant <nickel> faces and a high-impedance <voltage> measurement. Relative to the random-domain state,
$$
\varepsilon_x=\Lambda=-4\times10^{-5},\qquad
\varepsilon_y=-\Lambda/2=2\times10^{-5}.
$$
Treat the thin central layer as in <plane stress>, with these imposed in-plane strains, negligible converse-effect backreaction and isotropic elastic constants. Then
$$
\sigma_x=\frac{E}{1-\nu^2}(\varepsilon_x+\nu\varepsilon_y),
\quad
\sigma_y=\frac{E}{1-\nu^2}(\varepsilon_y+\nu\varepsilon_x),
$$
so
$$
\sigma_x+\sigma_y=\frac{E(\varepsilon_x+\varepsilon_y)}{1-\nu}
=\frac{E\Lambda}{2(1-\nu)}=-1.80\,\mathrm{MPa}.
$$
For a transversely polarized layer,
$$
D_3=d_{31}(\sigma_x+\sigma_y)+\epsilon_0\kappa E_3.
$$
The open-circuit increment has $\Delta D_3\simeq0$, and $|E_3|=|V|/t=800\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{|d_{31}|\simeq
\frac{\epsilon_0\kappa|V|}{t|\sigma_x+\sigma_y|}
=6.69\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{C\,N^{-1}}
=6.69\,\mathrm{pC\,N^{-1}}.}
$$
The observed <voltage> magnitude does not specify the sign without electrode polarity and poling direction. A one-dimensional approximation retaining only axial <stress> $E\Lambda$ instead gives $4.78\,\mathrm{pC\,N^{-1}}$. That is a different mechanical model. <Nickel> stiffness and thickness, interface compliance, central-layer tensor coefficients and electrical loading are not specified, so **the experiment does not determine a unique exact intrinsic coefficient without these assumptions**.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a conventional initially random, untextured piezoceramic, orientational averaging suppresses the macroscopic linear response. Electrical poling of <ferroelectric domains> is the usual way to generate a lasting net response. **Under that conventional preparation assumption, a ferroelectric constituent is the expected route.**

However, the word polycrystalline alone does not logically require <ferroelectricity>. A deliberately textured aggregate of nonswitchable piezoelectric grains can have a nonzero effective tensor if their orientations and polar directions do not cancel. <Ferroelectricity> means electrically switchable spontaneous polarization, whereas <piezoelectricity> means linear <stress>–charge coupling. Thus the unconditional statement that every polycrystalline piezoelectric must be ferroelectric is too strong.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No. A piezoelectric single crystal need not be ferroelectric.** A noncentrosymmetric crystal can couple <strain> to polarization without having a spontaneous polarization that an electric field can switch. Quartz is a standard example.

A single crystal already has an oriented crystallographic tensor, so the random-grain cancellation issue does not arise. The relevant tests are crystal symmetry and the nonzero piezoelectric coefficients; ferroelectric switching is a separate property.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**If removal restores the original random-domain magnetic state, the <magnetostrictive strain> and induced <voltage> return toward their original values.** Elastic <strain> transfer then relaxes the <stress> on the central layer.

A ferromagnetic <nickel> layer can retain <remanent magnetization> and an altered domain population, so removing the external field does not guarantee removal of all <magnetostriction>. The electrical time response also depends on leakage and the input impedance of the voltmeter: an open-circuit strain-induced <voltage> can decay through a finite resistance even while some <strain> remains. The final observed <voltage> is therefore history- and circuit-dependent.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**Reversing a saturating longitudinal field gives approximately the same <strain> and the same <voltage> polarity relative to the original unmagnetized reference.** In the isotropic orientational model,
$$
\varepsilon^{\rm mag}_{ij}=\frac{3\Lambda}{2}
\left(m_im_j-\frac{\delta_{ij}}3\right).
$$
It is unchanged under $\mathbf m\to-\mathbf m$. Both positive and negative longitudinal saturation therefore contract the negative-magnetostriction <nickel> in the same axial direction. The resulting <stress> on the piezoelectric layer has the same sign.

During field reversal there can be a hysteretic transient as domains reorganize, and finite circuit leakage can alter the measured waveform. Those effects do not turn the saturated <strain> law into a response odd in the field.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Assume that the transverse field is large enough to saturate through the thickness despite demagnetizing effects, and retain the thin-layer plane-stress and open-circuit assumptions from part(a). The two imposed in-plane strains now become
$$
\varepsilon_x=\varepsilon_y=-\Lambda/2=2\times10^{-5}.
$$
Their sum is $-\Lambda$, whereas it was $\Lambda/2$ for longitudinal saturation. The in-plane <stress> sum, and hence the induced open-circuit <voltage>, is multiplied by $-2$:
$$
\boxed{V_{\rm thickness}\simeq-2V_{\rm longitudinal}.}
$$
Thus **the magnitude is about $24\,\mathrm{mV}$ with the opposite polarity**, under this model. The larger demagnetizing field for an out-of-plane direction means the same external field magnitude might not produce the same degree of saturation. Changes in thickness constraint or <strain> transfer would also modify the estimate.

= 3
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a cubic phase, $d_{hkl}=a/\sqrt{h^2+k^2+l^2}$ and <Bragg's law> gives
$$
\sin^2\theta=\frac{\lambda^2}{4a^2}(h^2+k^2+l^2).
$$
The first six FCC-allowed squared-index sums are $3,4,8,11,12,16$, from indices all odd or all even. **The labels1–6 are therefore**
$$
\boxed{111,\quad200,\quad220,\quad311,\quad222,\quad400.}
$$
Remember that the plot reports $2\theta$, not $\theta$. The first peak gives
$$
a_{111}=\frac{\sqrt3(0.1542)}{2\sin(43.1^\circ/2)}
\simeq0.36356\,\mathrm{nm}.
$$
The last peak provides an independent estimate,
$$
a_{400}=\frac{4(0.1542)}{2\sin(115.9^\circ/2)}
\simeq0.36386\,\mathrm{nm}.
$$
Thus **$a\simeq0.3638\,\mathrm{nm}$** is appropriate to the supplied precision. The two estimates differ by about $0.08\%$; high-angle data are less sensitive to a fixed angular error, so the $400$ estimate is useful for the composition calculation. The printed angles do not justify many more significant figures or an exact fit of all six positions.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**Sample A is the Cu–Au alloy.** At the indicated copper-rich composition, the supplied Cu–Au diagram permits one copper-rich solid-solution phase. The Cu–Ag diagram instead gives two equilibrium solid phases at room <temperature>. A single consistent set of FCC peaks therefore identifies A under the stated equilibrium assumptions; similar pure-metal lattice parameters alone would not distinguish <gold> from silver.

Use <Vegard's law> as an approximate atomic-fraction interpolation:
$$
a\simeq(1-x_{\rm Au})a_{\rm Cu}+x_{\rm Au}a_{\rm Au}.
$$
With the higher-angle estimate from part(a),
$$
x_{\rm Au}=\frac{0.363857-0.3615}{0.4078-0.3615}\simeq0.0509.
$$
This is about $5.1$ atomic percent, not weight percent. Using rounded molar masses $M_{\rm Au}\simeq197$ and $M_{\rm Cu}\simeq63.55\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$,
$$
\boxed{w_{\rm Au}=\frac{x_{\rm Au}M_{\rm Au}}
{x_{\rm Au}M_{\rm Au}+(1-x_{\rm Au})M_{\rm Cu}}
\simeq0.143,\quad\text{about }14\,\mathrm{wt\%\ Au}.}
$$
Using the first peak alone instead gives roughly $12.6\,\mathrm{wt\%}$. The justified conclusion is therefore about **$13$–$14\,\mathrm{wt\%}$ <gold>**, with uncertainty from angle rounding, calibration and the approximate linear lattice-composition relation.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**Sample B should show two FCC peak families**, one from a copper-rich phase and one from a silver-rich phase. Their lattice parameters are set by the compositions of the coexisting phases, not by one <lattice parameter> at the overall alloy composition.

For corresponding <Miller indices> the silver-rich phase has a larger <lattice parameter> and hence a larger interplanar spacing, so its reflection occurs at smaller $2\theta$. Some reflections may overlap or be weak. Relative intensities depend on phase fractions, atomic scattering factors, absorption and texture; a raw peak-height ratio is not directly a <mass fraction>.

At room <temperature> the mutual solubilities in the supplied Cu–Ag diagram are small, so the two families would be near the elemental copper and silver positions. Slow cooling is important: a metastable supersaturated single phase would be a different interpretation. Quantitative phase or composition analysis would require calibrated <diffraction> and an appropriate refinement, not merely counting the visible peaks.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**SEM with <energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy>** can identify <gold> versus silver through their characteristic X-ray energies. Acquire spectra and maps from representative regions: two compositionally distinct regions would support the two-phase interpretation of B. With standards or validated matrix corrections it can estimate local compositions and, with representative sampling, an overall composition. Surface topography and the interaction volume limit spatial interpretation.

**<X-ray fluorescence>** provides a complementary more bulk-averaged elemental measurement. <Gold> and silver produce different characteristic lines; calibrated intensities can yield their mass fractions, with corrections for absorption, excitation and specimen geometry. It does not by itself determine whether an element occupies one or two crystallographic phases.

Both methods supply elemental information missing from a lattice-parameter-only identification. A purely morphological micrograph could reveal phase separation but would not alone give a reliable numerical chemical composition.

= 4
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**<Pearlite> is a microstructural arrangement of two phases, <ferrite> and <cementite>, rather than a single phase.** <Ferrite> is an iron-rich <solid solution>; <cementite> is the carbide $\mathrm{Fe_3C}$. A <pearlite> colony contains alternating lamellae of these constituents formed by transformation of <austenite>.

A <thermodynamic phase> has a specified <crystal structure> and composition range with locally homogeneous properties on the appropriate scale. The word <pearlite> instead describes how distinct phases are arranged. Its lamellar spacing and orientation can change while the identities of its two phases remain the same.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the line fraction occupied by the tall narrow ridges as an estimate of <cementite> <volume fraction>, assuming the line is representative and its etched height classification identifies the phases correctly. **The displayed profile gives roughly $17\,\mathrm{vol\%}$ <cementite> and $83\,\mathrm{vol\%}$ <ferrite>.**

An operational measurement at a height threshold near $75\,\mathrm{nm}$ gives four ridge widths totaling about $0.88\,\mathrm{\mu m}$ over a profile about $5.3\,\mathrm{\mu m}$ long:
$$
\boxed{f_{\rm cem}\simeq\frac{\sum_jw_j}{L}\simeq\frac{0.88}{5.3}\simeq0.17,
\qquad f_\alpha\simeq0.83.}
$$
Changing the threshold over a reasonable part of the high ridges gives roughly $16$–$18\%$, so this is an image-based estimate, not an exact phase analysis. The occasional low protrusions should not all be counted as full <cementite> lamellae merely because they are above a noisy baseline. The reference line and threshold convention must be stated.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For eutectoid <pearlite>, apply the <lever rule> between <ferrite> and <cementite> at the transformation <temperature>. Using standard approximate Fe–C values $C_e=0.76$, $C_\alpha=0.022$ and $C_{\rm cem}=6.67$ in weight percent carbon,
$$
\boxed{w_{\rm cem}=\frac{C_e-C_\alpha}{C_{\rm cem}-C_\alpha}
=\frac{0.76-0.022}{6.67-0.022}\simeq0.111,
\qquad w_\alpha\simeq0.889.}
$$
These are **mass fractions**. To compare with an unbiased geometric line fraction, convert using the phase densities:
$$
f_{\rm cem}=\frac{w_{\rm cem}/\rho_{\rm cem}}
{w_{\rm cem}/\rho_{\rm cem}+(1-w_{\rm cem})/\rho_\alpha}.
$$
With representative densities $\rho_{\rm cem}\simeq7.69$ and $\rho_\alpha\simeq7.87\,\mathrm{Mg\,m^{-3}}$, this gives about **$11.3\,\mathrm{vol\%}$ <cementite> and $88.7\,\mathrm{vol\%}$ <ferrite>**. Because the densities are similar, the mass and volume fractions differ only modestly. The phase-diagram values here are conventional approximations, not a claim that the exact 2018 Data Book has been recovered.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**The profile estimate and equilibrium lever-rule estimate need not agree exactly.** The height trace contains only four major ridges, making it a small sample of the microstructure. Pixel size, ridge-threshold choice, tip geometry, contamination, polishing damage and unequal etching change apparent widths. A finite-radius AFM tip can broaden the narrow raised <cementite> regions, biasing their inferred fraction upward.

The equilibrium calculation assumes eutectoid <pearlite> with the chosen carbon composition and tie-line values. Off-eutectoid <steel> can include proeutectoid <ferrite> or <cementite> outside the <pearlite>; alloying, carbon segregation and non-equilibrium cooling alter the simple Fe–C estimate. Carbon solubility also varies with <temperature>. The mass-to-volume conversion requires phase densities.

The observed difference between roughly $17\%$ ridge fraction and $11\%$ equilibrium phase volume is therefore a reason to examine sampling and the etched-surface measurement, not to <force> the image widths to match the <lever rule>. Multiple colonies, repeated calibrated traces and a phase-sensitive measurement would improve the comparison.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**<Ferrite> etches faster in <Nital>**, so it recedes and leaves <cementite> standing as the higher ridges. The identification is based on the surface-relief preparation, not on assuming that every bright feature in every microscopy mode is <cementite>. In the stated AFM image, brightness is a displayed height contrast.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The lamellar interfaces now make $60^\circ$ with the surface normal, or $30^\circ$ with the surface itself. **Draw inclined alternating plates rather than vertical stripes**, with preferentially etched <ferrite> below the more resistant <cementite>.

\Image[paper-1-pearlite.png]
{title=Original inclined-lamella etching geometry and illustrative AFM profile; the synthetic spacing and fractions are not a tracing of the source data}

The upper panel shows an inclined microstructure intersecting the original plane. Differential etching can expose a shallow inclined <cementite> face on one side of a ridge and an undercut or overhang on the other. The lower panel shows its accessible height profile, with additional rounding from a finite tip. It is therefore possible to obtain an asymmetric ridge even from an initially planar section through parallel lamellae.

For an ideal unetched planar cut, a plate of normal thickness $d$ has apparent surface width
$$
w=\frac{d}{\sin30^\circ}=2d.
$$
The same factor applies to the <ferrite> spacing. Inclination alone broadens all intersections together; it does not by itself change the true phase-width ratio.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**The danger is identifying an etched height interval with the original planar phase intersection.** Preferential <ferrite> dissolution exposes inclined <cementite> faces and can produce overhangs. A conventional AFM tip cannot follow a reentrant underside; its finite size also broadens ridge tops and may not enter the intervening troughs. A thresholded height width can consequently differ from both the true plate thickness and its original surface intersection.

A uniform inclination at an ideal flat cut does not itself invalidate a line-fraction estimate: both phases acquire the same geometrical width factor, which cancels in their ratio. The problem is differential relief and probe accessibility after etching, together with uncertain local angles and limited sampling. Using only the apparent ridge occupancy can overestimate <cementite>. An independently polished section, calibrated probe geometry, several directions and colonies, or another phase-sensitive method is needed to test the inference.

= 5
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume a thin cylindrical wall, uniform internal pressure and closed ends carrying the pressure load. The radial <stress> is small compared with the membrane stresses when $t\ll r$.

For an end cap, the outward pressure <force> is $P\pi r^2$. The wall's axial tensile <force> is $\sigma_z(2\pi rt)$. Equilibrium therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\sigma_z=\frac{Pr}{2t}.}
$$
For a longitudinally split half-cylinder of length $L$, pressure has resultant $P(2rL)$ across the projected rectangular area. Two cut wall sections each carry $\sigma_\theta tL$, so
$$
\boxed{\sigma_\theta=\frac{Pr}{t}=2\sigma_z.}
$$
Thus **<hoop stress> is twice the closed-end axial <stress>** in this model. An open-ended tube without an external end load does not acquire the same pressure-induced <axial membrane stress>.

\Image[paper-1-pipe-fracture.png]
{title=Original thin-pipe force balances and schematic crack-tip stress regularization by plastic deformation; the plastic-zone curve is qualitative}

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Linear elastic fracture mechanics predicts a near-tip tensile <stress> varying approximately as
$$
\sigma_{yy}(r,0)\simeq\frac{K_I}{\sqrt{2\pi r}}.
$$
**A ductile material yields near the tip before that mathematical singularity can be realized.** Plastic deformation blunts the crack and redistributes <stress> over a finite process zone, as shown in the right-hand diagram. Constraint can sustain stresses above the uniaxial yield <stress>, so the schematic plateau should not be interpreted as a universal exactly-$\sigma_y$ cap.

A useful process-zone scale is $r_p\sim(K_I/\sigma_y)^2$ with a geometry- and constraint-dependent coefficient. Advancing the crack then requires energy for plastic deformation and other irreversible processes as well as forming new surfaces. Consequently the critical energy release rate is much larger than the reversible surface-energy term $2\gamma$ of a perfectly brittle idealization:
$$
G_c\simeq2\gamma+G_{\rm plastic}+G_{\rm other}.
$$
In a small-scale-yielding plane-strain description, $G_c=K_{IC}^2/E'$ with $E'=E/(1-\nu^2)$. Large-scale yielding requires an elastic-plastic measure rather than treating a linear-elastic stress-intensity criterion as exact. The increased dissipation and crack-tip blunting explain the larger energy requirement.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $a$ be a characteristic crack depth and $Y$ its geometry factor. A simple fast-fracture condition is
$$
K_I=Y\sigma\sqrt{\pi a}=K_{IC},\qquad
 a_c=\frac1\pi\left(\frac{K_{IC}}{Y\sigma}\right)^2.
$$
The crack should penetrate a wall of thickness $t$ before reaching the critical size, so the simplified <leak-before-break criterion> is $t<a_c$, with appropriate geometry and stable-through-wall-crack assumptions.

**The conventional material-based maximum thickness uses the yield <stress> as a conservative limiting operating <stress>**:
$$
\boxed{t<t_{\max}^{(\sigma_y)}=
\frac1\pi\left(\frac{K_{IC}}{Y\sigma_y}\right)^2.}
$$
It ensures that the depth required for penetration is subcritical even at the stated limiting <stress>. Geometrical factors depend on crack shape and orientation; this is an educational screening estimate rather than a full crack-growth or leak-detection analysis. The handbook's fracture formula uses the same stress-intensity and geometry-factor convention.<Materials Data Book by Cambridge University Engineering Department>[2011 edition revised2019, printed p.6, Fast Fracture; this confirms the formula convention, not the exact2018NST supplied values]{ref}

There is an important distinction if the actual pressure and radius are fixed while thickness varies. Substituting $\sigma=Pr/t$ at penetration $a=t$ gives
$$
K_I(t)=YPr\sqrt{\frac\pi t},
\qquad
\boxed{t>\pi\left(\frac{YPr}{K_{IC}}\right)^2.}
$$
That is a **lower**, not upper, bound on thickness under the fixed-pressure model. Yield avoidance additionally requires $t\gtrsim Pr/\sigma_y$. It would be inconsistent to derive the fixed-pressure expression and call it a maximum. The standard maximum quoted above answers a different, yield-limited material-selection comparison.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <aluminium> alloy, write $K_{IC,\rm Al}$ and $\sigma_{y,\rm Al}$ for the appropriate data-book properties at the relevant material state and <temperature>. The yield-limited screening bounds from part(c), with $r\simeq0.70\,\mathrm m$, are
$$
\boxed{t_{\max,\rm Al}=\frac1\pi
\left(\frac{K_{IC,\rm Al}}{Y\sigma_{y,\rm Al}}\right)^2,
\qquad t_{\rm yield,\rm Al}\simeq\frac{7\,\mathrm{MPa\,m}}{\sigma_{y,\rm Al}}.}
$$
For $K$ in $\mathrm{MPa\sqrt m}$ and $\sigma_y$ in $\mathrm{MPa}$, the numerical substitutions are $t_{\max}(\mathrm{mm})=318.31[K/(Y\sigma_y)]^2$ and $t_{\rm yield}(\mathrm{mm})=7000/\sigma_y$.

A high-strength alloy's large yield <stress> can require a small allowable leak-before-break thickness even when its minimum pressure thickness is modest. FCC structure generally favors low-temperature plasticity, but does not by itself establish the alloy's <fracture toughness>, weld performance, corrosion behavior or fatigue resistance.

