= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For Ti–6Al–4V, use its own <fracture toughness> and yield <stress> in
$$
\boxed{t_{\max,\rm Ti}=\frac1\pi
\left(\frac{K_{IC,\rm Ti}}{Y\sigma_{y,\rm Ti}}\right)^2,
\qquad t_{\rm yield,\rm Ti}\simeq\frac{7\,\mathrm{MPa\,m}}{\sigma_{y,\rm Ti}}.}
$$
The same unit-conversion formulas from part(i) apply. A feasible yield-limited screening interval requires $t_{\rm yield}<t_{\max}$, using consistent crack geometry and low-temperature properties.

High specific strength and corrosion resistance can be attractive, but cost, fabrication, joining and toughness must also be assessed. The label HCP is not a sufficient rule that every <titanium> alloy has the same sharp <ductile-to-brittle transition> as ferritic <steel>; actual low-temperature alloy and microstructural data are needed.

**TODO: numerical thickness cannot be completed without the unavailable 2018 NST Data Book's Ti–6Al–4V property pair.** No historical value or material-specific Arctic toughness is invented.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For mild <steel>, the corresponding bounds are
$$
\boxed{t_{\max,\rm mild}=\frac1\pi
\left(\frac{K_{IC,\rm mild}}{Y\sigma_{y,\rm mild}}\right)^2,
\qquad t_{\rm yield,\rm mild}\simeq\frac{7\,\mathrm{MPa\,m}}{\sigma_{y,\rm mild}}.}
$$
A relatively low yield <stress> increases the thickness needed to carry pressure, but a favorable toughness-to-yield-strength ratio can give a much larger leak-before-break upper bound. This tradeoff often makes a tough structural <steel> more attractive than choosing the highest strength alone.

For Arctic service, the <ductile-to-brittle transition> is crucial. A room-temperature toughness cannot be assumed to persist below the transition, and welding, constraint and loading rate affect the comparison. A suitably qualified low-temperature <steel> can be practical; the word mild alone does not establish that qualification.

**TODO: numerical thickness cannot be completed without the unavailable 2018 NST Data Book values.** The <temperature> and state of the tabulated toughness must also be identified before making a numerical service claim.

= iv
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For high-strength <steel>, use
$$
\boxed{t_{\max,\rm high}=\frac1\pi
\left(\frac{K_{IC,\rm high}}{Y\sigma_{y,\rm high}}\right)^2,
\qquad t_{\rm yield,\rm high}\simeq\frac{7\,\mathrm{MPa\,m}}{\sigma_{y,\rm high}}.}
$$
At fixed toughness, raising yield <stress> reduces $t_{\max}$ quadratically but reduces the required pressure thickness only linearly. Thus a higher-strength grade can have a worse leak-before-break screening margin. The relevant screening combination is $K_{IC}^2/\sigma_y$, since simultaneous satisfaction requires
$$
\boxed{\frac{K_{IC}^2}{\sigma_y}>\pi Y^2Pr
\simeq22.0Y^2\,\mathrm{MPa\,m}.}
$$
Use measured low-temperature toughness and appropriate joining/environmental performance; strength alone does not establish suitability. The comparison also has to remain within the thin-wall approximation.

