= Solution
{parent=b}

Interpret the supplied real quantity as the <storage modulus> $E'$, with $k$ the <spring> modulus in this continuum representation and $\eta$ the corresponding <viscosity>. Let $\tau=t_R=\eta/k$. Then
$$
E'=\frac{k\omega^2\tau^2}{1+\omega^2\tau^2}.
$$
**At high frequency, $\omega\tau\gg1$, the response approaches the <spring> stiffness; at low frequency it becomes much softer**:
$$
\boxed{E'\simeq k\quad(\omega\tau\gg1),\qquad
E'\simeq k\omega^2\tau^2=\frac{\eta^2\omega^2}{k}
\quad(\omega\tau\ll1).}
$$
In the series <Maxwell model>, the <spring> and <dashpot> carry the same <stress> while their strains add. A rapidly changing load allows little viscous flow, so the <spring> dominates the <strain> response. Over a long cycle the <dashpot> has time to deform, giving a small <storage modulus> and a fluid-like low-frequency response. This is generic viscoelastic mechanics, independent of the biological parts deferred above.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

With $s=\omega^2$, the curve is
$$
E'(s)=\frac{k\tau^2s}{1+\tau^2s},\qquad
\frac{dE'}{ds}=\frac{k\tau^2}{(1+\tau^2s)^2}>0.
$$
**It rises linearly from zero, bends downward and approaches the plateau $k$**. Its half-height occurs at $s=\tau^{-2}$.

For $\omega^2\ll\tau^{-2}$, the <dashpot> contributes the larger <strain> and dominates the compliant response. For $\omega^2\gg\tau^{-2}$, <dashpot> deformation during a cycle is small relative to <spring> <strain>, and the response is spring-dominated. The crossover separates these regimes rather than a discontinuous transition.

\Image[paper-1-maxwell.png]
{title=Original Maxwell storage curves versus squared frequency and the distinct dissipation laws at fixed strain and fixed stress amplitudes; axes use logarithmic spacing to show both regimes}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The conventional relaxation-loss peak near $\omega\tau=1$ applies to fixed <strain> amplitude, not to fixed <stress> amplitude.** This distinction matters because the question explicitly describes oscillating <stress>.

The full <complex modulus> of the series Maxwell element is
$$
E^*=\frac{ki\omega\tau}{1+i\omega\tau},\qquad
E''=\frac{k\omega\tau}{1+\omega^2\tau^2}.
$$
For imposed <strain> $\varepsilon=\varepsilon_0\cos\omega t$, the energy dissipated per volume per cycle is
$$
W_\varepsilon=\pi E''\varepsilon_0^2.
$$
Since $x/(1+x^2)$ has its maximum at $x=1$, **fixed-strain loss per cycle peaks at $\omega^2=\tau^{-2}$**. At that scale the imposed cycle time is comparable with the <relaxation time>, and substantial viscous flow occurs while <stress> remains appreciable.

For the literally stated comparison with fixed <stress> amplitude, use the compliance
$$
J^*=\frac1k+\frac1{i\omega\eta}.
$$
The <dashpot> <strain> rate is $\dot\varepsilon_d=\sigma/\eta$. Its dissipation integrated over a period gives
$$
\boxed{W_\sigma=\int_0^{2\pi/\omega}\frac{\sigma_0^2\cos^2\omega t}{\eta}\,dt
=\frac{\pi\sigma_0^2}{\eta\omega}.}
$$
This decreases monotonically with frequency; **there is no finite-frequency maximum at fixed <stress> amplitude in the ideal <Maxwell model>**. Its mean dissipated <power> is $\sigma_0^2/(2\eta)$, independent of frequency. The formal low-frequency divergence in energy per cycle accompanies very large <strain> excursions and cannot be extrapolated beyond the linear model's range. The graph identifies both results rather than assigning the fixed-strain peak to the wrong loading condition.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Within the rubbery regime, **<viscosity> usually decreases as <temperature> increases**, because segmental rearrangement becomes easier. The <spring>'s entropic contribution generally increases approximately with absolute <temperature> at fixed effective network density, so $k$ tends to increase modestly under that assumption.

Consequently $\tau=\eta/k$ decreases. The storage-modulus crossover moves to larger squared frequency,
$$
\omega_{\rm cross}^2=\tau^{-2},
$$
and the high-frequency plateau moves upward if $k$ increases. The warmer curve in the figure illustrates those two changes. At intermediate frequencies the reduced <relaxation time> can lower the <storage modulus> even though its eventual plateau is higher; there is no requirement that the entire curve move upward.

These trends assume the same rubbery material state and no degradation, crystallization or change in effective network density. Close to other transitions the simple entropic-spring and <viscosity> picture needs modification.

= 7
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**A <displacive transformation> changes structure through coordinated atomic displacements, whereas a <reconstructive transformation> changes the bonding/connectivity arrangement more substantially.**

In a displacive change, neighboring atoms move cooperatively without the long-range compositional redistribution characteristic of diffusion-controlled precipitation. The change can preserve many near-neighbor relationships and can occur rapidly when its kinetic conditions are met.

A reconstructive change requires breaking and re-forming the relevant structural connections to establish a different arrangement, often with a larger activation barrier and slower nucleation and growth. It may consequently show substantial kinetic persistence of a metastable phase. The distinction concerns the atomic mechanism, not merely whether the final phase has a different symmetry; neither label by itself supplies a universal transformation rate.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For two phases of the same pure substance at equilibrium, their chemical potentials are equal. Along their coexistence boundary,
$$
\mu_1=\mu_2,\qquad d\mu=-s_m\,dT+v_m\,dp.
$$
Differentiating the equality gives
$$
-s_{m,1}\,dT+v_{m,1}\,dp
=-s_{m,2}\,dT+v_{m,2}\,dp.
$$
Therefore the <Clapeyron equation> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{dp}{dT}=\frac{\Delta s_m}{\Delta v_m}
=\frac{\Delta s}{\Delta v}
=\frac{L}{T\Delta v}.}
$$
Molar or mass-specific quantities give the same slope if a consistent basis is used. $L=T\Delta s$ follows from equal Gibbs free energies at coexistence.

Solid phases are relatively incompressible and have modest <thermal expansion> over many intervals. If their <entropy> and volume differences vary slowly enough that $\Delta s/\Delta v$ is approximately constant, the boundary is approximately straight. **Straightness is an approximation, not a thermodynamic requirement.** Heat-capacity differences, pressure dependence of volume, other transitions and proximity to special points can curve the boundary. The ideal-vapor integrated Clausius-Clapeyron approximation is not appropriate for these two solid phases.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Read the <coesite>–<stishovite> boundary at approximately $(400\,{}^\circ\mathrm C,7.2\,\mathrm{GPa})$ and $(2800\,{}^\circ\mathrm C,13.5\,\mathrm{GPa})$. The slope is therefore
$$
\frac{dp}{dT}\simeq\frac{(13.5-7.2)\times10^9}{2800-400}
=2.625\times10^6\,\mathrm{Pa\,K^{-1}}.
$$
A Celsius <temperature> difference equals the corresponding kelvin difference. The graph does not justify treating the endpoints as exact measurements.

For <stishovite>, the tetragonal cell volume divided by two gives
$$
v_{{\rm formula},s}=\frac{a^2c}{2}
=0.0232486\,\mathrm{nm^3}.
$$
For monoclinic <coesite> the cell volume is $abc\sin\beta$, so
$$
v_{{\rm formula},c}=\frac{(0.7143)^2(1.2383)\sin120^\circ}{16}
=0.0341978\,\mathrm{nm^3}.
$$
Using $M_{\mathrm{SiO_2}}\simeq0.060084\,\mathrm{kg\,mol^{-1}}$ and Avogadro's constant, the mass-specific volumes are
$$
v_s\simeq2.3302\times10^{-4},\qquad
v_c\simeq3.4276\times10^{-4}\quad\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}}.
$$
For the direction **<stishovite> to <coesite>**,
$$
\boxed{\Delta s_{s\to c}=(v_c-v_s)\frac{dp}{dT}
\simeq+2.88\times10^2\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}.}
$$
For the reverse, <coesite> to <stishovite>, the <entropy> change is **about $-288\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$**. <Stishovite> is the denser phase: a positive boundary slope and its smaller volume imply a lower <entropy> on the high-pressure side. Stating the direction prevents an otherwise avoidable sign ambiguity.
